# Every variable this service reads, and nothing else.
#
# Rule 9 of docs/ecosystem/03 §2: a repository declares the variables it needs and the deploy
# provides exactly those. `env_file: .env` fan-out — which hands every container the whole
# estate's secrets — is banned, and this file is what makes the alternative possible: the deploy
# manifest is derived from it.
#
# Copy to `.env` for local work. `.env` is gitignored and must stay that way.

# --- required --------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# This service's own database. It reads no other, and CI fails on a second connection string.
ACTIVITY_DATABASE_URL=postgres://cloudsforge:CHANGE_ME@127.0.0.1:5432/activity

# ── SERVING BOTH NETWORKS FROM ONE POD ────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Both OPTIONAL and empty here: a development stack is one network. See
# micro-deploy `docs/network-consolidation.md`.
#
# Set the second DSN and this deployment holds a handle per network, choosing per
# request from the `CF-Network` header the gateway stamps. Empty, `networkSql`
# holds one handle and REFUSES a testnet request rather than answering it out of
# mainnet rows.
ACTIVITY_DATABASE_URL_TESTNET=
# ── WHICH ESTATE THIS DEPLOYMENT IS ───────────────────────────────────────────
#
# REQUIRED in the estate, and the render sets it for every pod. It was once
# documented here as "never in production" and that was wrong twice over:
#
#   1. The pod keys its own database handle by it. Without it a testnet
#      deployment registers its testnet DSN under the name `mainnet` and then
#      refuses every request stamped `CF-Network: testnet`.
#
#   2. SERVICE-TO-SERVICE CALLS NEVER REACH THE GATEWAY. An outbox relay POSTing
#      to another service goes container to container; nothing stamps a header
#      on it and nothing can. Without a declared network those answer 500.
#
# The header still WINS when present, so this cannot mask a mis-stamped request
# from outside — it only answers the internal callers that never had one. Leave
# it empty only where a pod genuinely serves both estates.
CF_NETWORK_SINGLE=


# Identity's JWKS and issuer. Token verification is the only thing this service asks identity for.
IDENTITY_JWKS_URL=http://127.0.0.1:4001/.well-known/jwks.json
IDENTITY_ISSUER=http://127.0.0.1:4001

# HMAC keys accepted on POST /ingest, newest first, comma separated. Generate each one:
#
#   openssl rand -base64 48
#
# A LIST, not one value: rotation must not require every producer in the estate and this service
# to change in the same instant. Publish the new secret, accept both for a window, then drop the
# old one — `verifyDelivery` in contracts-events reports which key matched, so an operator can see
# when the window can be closed.
#
# The boot guard checks the SHAPE of each entry rather than a list of known-bad strings: base64 or
# hex only (no hyphens — every placeholder this estate ever wrote had one), at least 32 DECODED
# BYTES rather than 24 keystrokes, and a measured entropy floor. So a typed value is refused however
# long it is, and there is no NODE_ENV exemption (micro-org #142). EVERY entry faces that bar,
# including the key on its way OUT of a rotation: that is the one an attacker already holds if it
# leaked. Do not paste a placeholder back in — it will not boot, and that is the point.
ACTIVITY_INGEST_SECRETS=CHANGE_ME_run_openssl_rand_base64_48

# --- optional, with the defaults shown ---------------------------------------------------------

PORT=4000
NODE_ENV=development
LOG_LEVEL=info
ACTIVITY_DATABASE_POOL_MAX=10

# How much clock skew and relay delay a delivery signature may carry. The default is
# DELIVERY_TOLERANCE_MS from contracts-events. Widening it makes a captured request a longer-lived
# credential, so it is a knob to turn down rather than up.
# ACTIVITY_DELIVERY_TOLERANCE_MS=300000

# How long a processed inbox row is kept. Must outlive every producer's retry horizon: pruned too
# early, a redelivery is processed as new. The unique constraint on source_event_id still catches
# it, which is why that constraint is in the schema and not only in the application.
# ACTIVITY_INBOX_RETENTION_DAYS=30

# How long a RECORD is kept, by retention class, in days. The periods and the lawful basis for each
# are in src/retention.ts; these four lines are the deployment's half of it.
#
# NOTHING NEEDS TO BE SET. The defaults ARE the policy, so an estate that configures none of this
# still enforces a period rather than silently enforcing none.
#
# EACH MAXIMUM IS ITS DEFAULT. A deployment may SHORTEN a period — a stricter jurisdiction, a
# tenant who asked for less — and may never lengthen one, so the numbers below are an upper bound
# on what any deployment of this service retains. Setting one higher is refused by name at boot.
#
# financial (deposits, withdrawals, transfers, conversions, trades, listings, tokens, wallet and
# treasury movements, billing): five years, an AML/CTF record-keeping obligation. The floor is a
# year rather than a week because shortening this is a decision for a lawyer, not for a variable.
# ACTIVITY_RETENTION_FINANCIAL_DAYS=1825
#
# personal (account, security, ownership, reward, community, governance, api): two years. The
# product promise of a durable timeline, and a legitimate interest in reconstructing an account
# takeover — a compromise is found in months, not years.
# ACTIVITY_RETENTION_PERSONAL_DAYS=730
#
# operational (records with no owner and no financial content): thirteen months, which is a full
# year of comparisons plus the month it takes to close one.
# ACTIVITY_RETENTION_OPERATIONAL_DAYS=400
#
# quarantine (everything in `unclassified`): ninety days, and a forcing function. A quarantined row
# exists so a topic this build predates can be reclassified later; a topic nobody ever classified
# must not become a permanent store of a payload nobody has ever read.
# ACTIVITY_RETENTION_QUARANTINE_DAYS=90

# Names this replica in jobs.locked_by. Defaults to the hostname, which is the container id under
# compose and the pod name under Kubernetes.
# INSTANCE_ID=

# The image tag, reported on every log line and in the release manifest.
# CLOUDSFORGE_TAG=dev

# --- not read by this service ------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# OUTBOX_SIGNING_SECRET is absent deliberately. Activity is a pure consumer — contracts-events
# registers no `activity.*` topic — so it publishes nothing and holds no signing key.
#
# OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT and the rest of OTEL_* are read by the OpenTelemetry SDK loaded
# ahead of the process, not by src/env.ts. They belong in the deploy, not here.
#
# ACTIVITY_TEST_DATABASE_URL is read only by the test suite, and only when its name contains
# "test" — the database-backed tests truncate.
